# Kabyle am surfaces.
#
# The everyday spoken frame is the "morning" noun in the genitive, "n
# tṣebḥit", colloquially "n ṣṣbeḥ".  @athmanemokraoui, native Kabyle speaker,
# on https://github.com/TigreGotico/chronologia/pull/846#issuecomment-5573978767:
#   "'it is ten in the morning' is 'd lɛecṛa n tṣebḥit' (or colloquially
#    'd lɛecṛa n ṣṣbeḥ')."
# kab.wikipedia writes the same frame with the emphatic ṣ, matching
# daypart_morning.voc:
#   "ɣef lweḥda n tṣ̣ebḥit : tebda-d temhelt n Mizrana" (Tafsut n Yimaziɣen)
#   "Lɛecṛa n tṣ̣ebḥit. Ldzayer tamaneɣt, deg Tsenbert ..." (idem)
# The plain-s "ssbaḥ" is attested in a quoted song text:
#   "Neffeɣ-d ɣef lxemsa n ssbaḥ" (Agarsafen)
n tṣebḥit
n tṣ̣ebḥit
n taṣebḥit
tṣebḥit
taṣebḥit
n ṣṣbeḥ
ṣṣbeḥ
n ssbaḥ
ssbaḥ
# "ssbeḥ" is the surface this file shipped with and has no attestation of its
# own.  It collides with no other Kabyle word, so it stays as a tolerated
# variant rather than being dropped.
n ssbeḥ
ssbeḥ
# "n tufat" is CLDR 47's dayPeriods am for kab (cldr-dates-full/main/kab/
# ca-gregorian.json), and kab.wikipedia writes it in running text:
#   "Di 7 n tufat n wass n 8 yennayer" (Imenɣi n Lezzayer (1956-1957))
# It belongs to a FORMAL register.  @athmanemokraoui, in the same comment:
#   "Tufat is a modern standard not used in everyday native speech."
# That describes register, not existence: CLDR is a real attestation and a
# user who writes "n tufat" is writing Kabyle, so it keeps parsing.  The forms
# a speaker actually says are "n tṣebḥit" and "n ṣṣbeḥ" above.
n tufat
tufat
